# keyaction.keys -- exercises step 6 of build/plans/isocline.rst: the
# sdb-side "?"/ESC-//ESC-. key bindings, built on the generic
# ic_keyaction_replace()/ic_bind_key() mechanism from steps 4/5.
# CTRL-G (dismiss completions) reuses the mechanism already fully
# exercised by keybind.keys, so it is not retested here.  Driven
# through the jig's own fake bindings; see jig_help_action/
# jig_dabbrev_action/jig_lastarg_action in jig.c.  The token-matching
# logic itself (quote-scan, proper-prefix extension, last-token) is
# covered headlessly by tests/sdb/keyaction.cmd against sdb's actual
# isocline-agnostic functions; this file only exercises the keystroke
# path a real raw-mode loop requires.
#
# Part 1 ("?"): inside an open quote, "?" self-inserts instead of
# triggering completion, so quoted text isn't interrupted -- the line
# ends up literally containing "?".
i 2
+ "ab
+ ?
k ENTER
w 20
#
# Outside a quote, "?" prints the fake completer's matches for the
# word up to the cursor without inserting anything or disturbing the
# line -- "show v" narrows its three fixed commands down to just
# "show version".
+ show v
+ ?
k ENTER
w 20
#
# Part 2 (ESC-/): two history entries share a "pr"-prefixed word in
# different positions.  Submitted oldest first, so the dabbrev search
# (newest history entry first) hits "print exactly" before "previous
# print thing".
+ previous print thing
k ENTER
w 20
+ print exactly
k ENTER
w 20
#
# First press expands "pr" to the nearer match ("print"); a second
# press on the same prefix cycles further back to "previous".  A
# third press on the same line has no further match (both history
# words are now consumed), so the line is left unchanged.
+ pr
k E-/
w 20
k E-/
w 20
k E-/
w 20
k ENTER
w 20
#
# Part 3 (ESC-.): the most recent history entry right now ("previous",
# from the line just submitted) has a single token and is skipped;
# ESC-. inserts the last token of the next entry back, "print exactly",
# giving "exactly".
+ result:
k E-.
w 20
k ENTER
w 20
q
